--TEST--
Registering a shutdown handler asks for no shutdown, and the drain it will run inside is still `Core\Server::isDraining`'s to report — `rule:core-api/tier-roster`, `rule:concurrency/a-drain-closes-a-connection-cleanly`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Signal` composes with the drain that already exists rather than
// carrying a second one, and the two halves of that are asked here in order.
// First: this program has begun no shutdown, so the one bit both classes read
// answers `false`.
if (Core\Server::isDraining()) {
    echo "draining\n";
} else {
    echo "accepting\n";
}

// Second, and the part only this case asks: registering a handler is not a way
// to start what the handler is for. A class that began the drain when it took
// the callable — or that kept a bit of its own for the handler to run inside —
// changes the answer below, which is the same question asked again with a
// registration between the two askings.
Core\Signal::onShutdown(fn (): void => {
    echo "shutting down\n";
});

if (Core\Server::isDraining()) {
    echo "draining after registering\n";
} else {
    echo "accepting after registering\n";
}

// Asked with no `nvs.toml` and no grant: a shutdown is a fact about the process
// rather than a door, so nothing has to be configured or granted for a program
// to say what it would like to run when one begins.
echo "registered with nothing configured\n";
--EXPECT--
accepting
accepting after registering
registered with nothing configured
